description:
Add a new method on `MetadataBackend` carrying the metadata-specific filter args (`filterRelatedType`, `filterMetadataType`) from draft-ietf-jmap-metadata-01 §3.3, with a default impl that delegates to `JmapBackend::get_changes::<Metadata>` (preserving current behavior bit-for-bit). Refactor `handle_metadata_changes` in metadata.rs to call the new method instead of `handle_changes` directly.

This is the non-breaking shape change. No MemoryBackend changes here — those live in .5.2.

Sites:
  - crate-jmap-metadata-server/src/backend.rs — add `get_metadata_changes` method on `MetadataBackend` trait
  - crate-jmap-metadata-server/src/metadata.rs:61 — refactor `handle_metadata_changes`

Trait method shape:

    /// Return changes since `since_state` filtered by the metadata-specific
    /// filter args from draft §3.3 (`filterRelatedType`, `filterMetadataType`).
    ///
    /// Default impl delegates to [`JmapBackend::get_changes`], ignoring the
    /// filter args; the handler then post-filters `created` and `updated`
    /// by re-fetching objects via `get_objects::<Metadata>`. The `destroyed`
    /// array is returned unfiltered under the default impl — strict §3.3
    /// conformance requires overriding this method (see .5.2).
    ///
    /// Backends that index their change log on (relatedType, @type) SHOULD
    /// override to pre-filter at the storage layer, which gains strict
    /// §3.3 conformance for the destroyed array and removes the per-Id
    /// re-fetch cost for created/updated.
    fn get_metadata_changes(
        &self,
        caller: &Self::CallerCtx,
        account_id: &jmap_types::Id,
        since_state: &jmap_types::State,
        max_changes: Option<u64>,
        filter_related_type: Option<&str>,
        filter_metadata_type: Option<&[String]>,
    ) -> impl std::future::Future<Output = Result<ChangesResult, BackendChangesError<Self::Error>>> + Send {
        let _ = (filter_related_type, filter_metadata_type);
        self.get_changes::<jmap_metadata_types::Metadata>(
            caller, account_id, since_state, max_changes,
        )
    }

Handler refactor:
  - `handle_metadata_changes` should still parse the filter args off `args` (current code at metadata.rs:69-92 is correct as-is).
  - Instead of delegating to `jmap_server::handlers::handle_changes::<Metadata, B>` and post-filtering, call `backend.get_metadata_changes(...)` and then build the JMAP wire response from the returned `ChangesResult`. Post-filter for `created`/`updated` becomes the default-impl path; backends that override get strict conformance for free.
  - Update the doc comment at metadata.rs:49-60: replace 'Strict conformance with §3.3 for the destroyed case requires a backend extension method ...' with 'Backends that override `get_metadata_changes` get strict §3.3 conformance for all three arrays. The default impl preserves the partial-conformance behavior documented above.'

## Acceptance
  - `cargo test --workspace` clean — existing tests pass (default impl preserves current handler-level behavior bit-for-bit; the existing changes_filter_related_type_drops_non_matching_created test still passes against MockBackend).
  - `cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings` clean.
  - `cargo doc -p jmap-metadata-server --no-deps` clean (RUSTDOCFLAGS='-D warnings').
  - The handler doc comment cites draft §3.3 and explains the default-vs-override split.
  - No changes to memory.rs (out of scope; .5.2 will override).

## Out of scope
  - MemoryBackend strict-conformance override (→ .5.2)
  - ChangeEntry extension to carry per-Id (related_type, type_name) (→ .5.2)
  - Tests for destroyed-array filtering (→ .5.2)

## Depends on
  JMAP-06zp.3.3 and JMAP-06zp.3.4 (both closed).
  Parent: JMAP-06zp.3.5.
